thanks Michael this is the AI briefing deep dive for Wednesday October 28 merging

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:54.720
the labs safety mechanism or moat I'm Bella like the briefing it is written

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:57.220
written and read by AI from public sources.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:03.500
Last week, the information reported that Sam Altman walked OpenAI staff

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:06.620
through three endgames for building advanced AI safely.

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:10.640
The first is an international inspector modeled on the IAEA,

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:12.680
the UN's nuclear watchdog.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:16.720
The second folds the leading labs into a single CERN-style entity.

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:20.960
In the third, one firm reaches self-improving AI first,

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:25.060
and in a phrase that goes back to the safety theorist Eliezer Yudkowsky,

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:27.500
melts everyone else's GPUs.

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:32.380
Altman reportedly called that one terrible and favored the inspector.

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:33.860
The timing matters.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:36.620
In September, Dario Amode's essay,

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:39.500
We Must Pace the Frontier drew public support from Altman,

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:41.540
Elon Musk, and Demi's Hassabis.

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:45.480
Within a week, paying subscribers sued all four companies,

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:48.280
calling it an illegal cartel to limit output.

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:52.760
By mid-October, Google and Meta were shipping new frontier models again.

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:54.780
So today's question is narrow.

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:59.200
If labs cannot agree to slow down without legal trouble,

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:03.500
is merging them a safety mechanism or a moat dressed up as one?

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:07.100
Start with what was actually said.

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:08.820
The report is paywalled.

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:13.760
As relayed by Forbes and others, it describes options the industry has discussed,

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:15.960
not a plan OpenAI has adopted.

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:21.480
The merger idea is labeled the Demi's plan, after Google DeepMind's Demi's Hassabis.

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:25.380
Beyond a single entity supplying models to partner countries,

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:27.300
its specifics are not stated.

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:29.940
Why would anyone want to merge labs at all?

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:31.900
Because of a coordination problem.

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:37.740
Each lab might privately prefer to go slower, but holding back alone is costly.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:43.520
Luisa Rodriguez of 80,000 Hours, a group focused on AI risk, puts it this way.

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:47.700
Every week a company holds back, its rivals can release a better model,

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:51.460
winning over more customers, more investors, and the best researchers.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:53.380
There are three ways out.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:57.540
Appoint a referee, merge into one team, or let one player win.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:11:59.120
Here is a worked example.

00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:04.860
Imagine four airlines agree to fly fewer routes so they can fix safety problems.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:09.520
However sincere the reason, that is an agreement between competitors to cut output.

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:12.300
The lawsuit against the labs uses this framing.

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:17.560
Its words are an agreement among competitors about how fast their competing products will improve.

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:20.140
Now merge the four airlines into one.

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:23.360
Cutting roots becomes one company's scheduling decision.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:25.480
Nothing about the restraint changes.

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:27.920
What changes is the legal category?

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:30.500
That is the core mechanism.

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:36.740
Consolidation turns a coordination problem that antitrust law polices into an internal choice.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:40.600
It is also exactly why consolidation is a competition problem.

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:44.820
The merged entity sets the pace and the price for everyone downstream.

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:48.960
The CERN analogy carries some weight, but less than it seems.

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:53.000
CERN works because a particle collider is one shared instrument,

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:55.500
and nobody sells collisions to consumers.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:59.720
Frontier models are products sold to millions of people,

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:01.880
with whole companies built on top of them.

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:04.280
A CERN for AI would be a supplier,

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:08.340
and the phrase partner countries suggests everyone outside it is a customer.

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:12.460
One thread runs through all three plans, and that is compute.

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:17.220
The inspector plan reportedly involves monitoring compute and energy use.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:20.680
The winner-takes-all plan is about starving rivals of chips,

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:25.080
whoever controls access to compute controls who counts as a frontier lab.

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:29.800
Here is what is established and how we know it.

00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:31.720
The lawsuit is a court record.

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:35.140
Bloomerg Law reports it as Buist v. Anthropic,

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:38.700
filed on September 18 in the Northern District of California.

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:44.720
It brings a Sherman Act claim against Anthropic, OpenAI, SpaceX AI, and Google,

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:47.880
and calls the conduct an output-restricting cartel.

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:51.760
The public endorsements of Amo Day's essay are on the record.

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:57.920
On September 29, the White House had the leaders of six companies sign a voluntary joint commitment

00:13:57.920 --> 00:13:59.400
on frontier responsibilities.

00:14:00.140 --> 00:14:01.660
It is not legally enforceable.

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:08.380
separately the information reported that google open ai and anthropic plan a self-regulatory body

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:14.480
called the standards authority for frontier ai it is modeled on finra the private body that

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:21.260
polices u.s brokerages the next web notes that reports do not name microsoft meta or x ai as

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:28.040
members altman's preference for an iaea style body is not new he has argued for one publicly

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:33.800
since May 2023. The staff meeting itself is a different kind of evidence. It is an internal

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:40.020
remark reported by a paywalled outlet, then relayed by others. The relays even disagree on

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:45.480
the report's date by a couple of days. There is no transcript, and OpenAI has not published

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:52.080
anything on it. Now for what is claimed but not shown. Nobody has shown that a merged lab would

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:57.440
be safer than several competing ones. Nobody has shown it would actually go slower, rather than

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:02.740
simply face fewer rivals. Membership is not stated. How open-weight developers would be

00:15:02.740 --> 00:15:08.700
treated is not stated. Any compute threshold is not stated. One relay says a decision on which

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:14.040
path to take is due within a year, but it is unclear who would be deciding. The voluntary

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:21.060
slowdown also gives us a small natural experiment. Crypto Briefing reports it largely unraveled

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:27.020
within about a month, once Google and Meta released new models. That suggests self-restraint

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:32.000
without enforcement is fragile. It does not tell us whether merging would be any sturdier.

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:38.600
The same facts read differently depending on how close you think dangerous capability is.

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:44.700
The AGI forward reading takes race dynamics as the main risk. If systems are close to dangerous,

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:50.980
fewer actors means fewer places to inspect and less pressure to cut corners. Writing in Lawfare,

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:56.160
Nicholas Felstead argues that competitive pressures will drive a race to the bottom on safety.

00:15:56.160 --> 00:16:01.200
He proposes narrow legal safe harbors so labs can coordinate pauses.

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:04.800
The strongest form of this view is practical.

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:07.620
You cannot meaningfully inspect 50 labs.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:13.180
The A.A. Realist reading starts from the absence of evidence that runaway capability is imminent.

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:18.660
On that view, a slowdown mostly restrains product quality for paying customers,

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:20.740
which is what the plaintiffs allege.

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:24.560
Consolidation then entrenches whoever is already ahead.

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:31.400
Fred Ashton of the American Action Forum, a center-right policy group, warns that incumbents

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:36.680
can use sweeping antitrust exemptions to create a moat that solidifies their market position.

00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:42.500
The market reading notes who benefits from the rules. The proposed standards body has three

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:48.660
founding labs, the slowdown held only until rivals saw an opening. The policy reading points

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:54.900
to a cleaner path. Rodriguez argues that government set the rules, so they apply to

00:16:54.900 --> 00:17:00.940
every company at once, and nobody has to be the one to go first. That favors the inspector over

00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:06.000
the merger. To be fair, it is also the option Altman reportedly says he prefers.

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:13.180
Our read is this. Of the three endgames, consolidation is the one where the safety

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:18.560
benefit is only asserted, while the competitive effect follows automatically. A merger

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:24.600
removes rivals by definition. Whether it lowers risk depends on governance nobody has described.

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:30.720
That does not make the underlying safety concern fake. It makes this particular remedy the one to

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:37.100
read most skeptically. Altman deserves credit for favoring the inspector, but an inspector's

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:43.140
design still decides who is inside the tent. For builders, the near-term risk is probably not a

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:49.240
merger. It is thresholds and membership. Rules that bite above some level of compute or energy

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:55.440
will define who counts as frontier. Challenger labs and open-weight releases will sit on one

00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:02.060
side of that line or the other. For companies built on frontier APIs, fewer suppliers would

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:07.580
mean less pricing power and less control over the roadmap. One question worth exploring in

00:18:07.580 --> 00:18:13.080
diligence might be this. If frontier supply narrowed to one or two providers or release

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:17.940
pace were capped, what would happen to this company's margins? Could it switch to a second

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:24.400
model source, including open waits within a quarter? What would change our mind is a published

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:29.940
design with open membership, independent inspectors, and evidence that slowing down

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:37.000
actually reduced incidents. Three things would settle a lot of this. First, the Trump

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:42.140
administration's superintelligence oversight framework, which reports say is now being drafted.

00:18:42.840 --> 00:18:47.900
The questions are whether it uses IAEA-style inspection or licensing, and where any compute

00:18:47.900 --> 00:18:53.920
line sits. Second, the antitrust case. A ruling on a motion to dismiss would tell us whether

00:18:53.920 --> 00:18:59.960
courts weigh safety packs on their actual effects. If they do, labs could coordinate without merging.

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:08.880
third the standards bodies charter expected at the end of 2026 or early 2027 the questions are

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:14.480
who gets in whether open weight developers can be certified and whether it has any real power

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:19.100
within about a year we should know whether this talk has turned into a structure

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:26.480
here's steven's take regulatory strategies for world dominance seems timely in the usa

